{"records":[{"id":"zhpg8xshp9","title":"Lawrence et al., PNAS 2026","description":"Data from “Lawrence et al., 2026 A Genome-Wide Screen Identifies that PLCG2 Restrains Lysosomal GCase Activity.\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-06-22T18:23:16.389Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/zhpg8xshp9","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/zhpg8xshp9"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Baris","last_name":"Bingol","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Natural Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"y8dndzn3mg","title":"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication","description":"These data are part of the Supplemental Items associated with the manuscript entitled \"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication\". These files include Supplementary Data file 1-4.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-03-16T10:34:13.579Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/y8dndzn3mg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/y8dndzn3mg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Limeng","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virus","Screening","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nnxh5d35nd","title":"Genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 screen identified KLF11 as a druggable suppressor for sarcoma cancer stem cells","description":"The original, unprocessed data alongside the paper","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-05-31T01:39:45.722Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nnxh5d35nd","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nnxh5d35nd"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Le","last_name":"Qu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ztg7pxr6ch","title":"STT3A is essential for Wnt signaling and represents a target for cancers driven by RNF43 deficiency","description":"This dataset contains the original raw and analyzed data supporting the manuscript “STT3A is essential for Wnt signaling and represents a target for cancers driven by RNF43 deficiency”. The study identifies STT3A as an essential regulator of the Wnt signaling pathway and a potential therapeutic target for RNF43-deficient cancers. Included data were generated from key experiments, including Wnt reporter assays (DDT), Western blotting (WB), and CRISPR screen, and other experiments central to the study","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-10-13T11:26:58.874Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ztg7pxr6ch","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ztg7pxr6ch"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Zhengjin","last_name":"He","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"mmnwww6n3s","title":"LZTFL1 Rewires NADPH–Glutathione Metabolism to Amplify Ferroptosis","description":"Ferroptosis, an iron-dependent form of regulated cell death, represents a therapeutic vulnerability in apoptosis-resistant cancers. Here, we identify leucine zipper transcription factor-like 1 (LZTFL1) as a key regulator of ferroptosis that rewires glutathione (GSH) metabolism. A genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 screen and multi-omics analysis revealed that LZTFL1 promotes oxidation of glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD), limiting NADPH production and GSH regeneration. GSH depletion in turn enhanced LZTFL1 translation via an AKT–mTOR–eIF4E pathway, establishing a feed-forward loop that amplifies ferroptosis. In vivo, the LZTFL1–FHOD1–G6PD axis sensitized xenograft and patient-derived xenograft models to ferroptosis, correlating with lipid peroxidation, reduced GSH, and extended survival. Clinically, LZTFL1 expression restored cisplatin efficacy in resistant lung and ovarian cancer cells and predicted better survival outcomes in LUAD patients. Moreover, FDA-approved compounds upregulated LZTFL1 and re-sensitized resistant tumors to cisplatin. These findings define LZTFL1 as both a biomarker and therapeutic target for enhancing ferroptosis-based cancer therapy. The files below contain raw western blot images and flow cytometry data from this study.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-06-12T06:59:22.445Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/mmnwww6n3s","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/mmnwww6n3s"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"XIANGFEI","last_name":"XUE","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot","Flowmetry"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"yzbfm4v7y7","title":"Identification of novel antiviral drug candidates using an optimized SARS-CoV-2 phenotypic screening platform. Bojkova et al","description":"This table presents the results from a screen of kinase inhibitors for activity against a SARS-CoV-2 Delta isolate. Caco-2-F03 cells were infected with an MOI of 0.01 and virus infection was monitored using caspase 3/7 activity as read-out.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-01-04T09:41:42.535Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/yzbfm4v7y7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/yzbfm4v7y7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Martin","last_name":"Michaelis","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Pharmacology","Virology","Drug Screening"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"pnbjdtdkfg","title":"A genome-scale gain-of-function CRISPR screen in CD8 T cells identifies proline metabolism as a means to enhance CAR-T therapy ","description":"CyTOF raw data.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-02-14T13:21:30.568Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/pnbjdtdkfg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/pnbjdtdkfg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lupeng","last_name":"Ye","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jonathan","last_name":"Park","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sidi","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Immunology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"tkh39ck93y","title":"A genome-wide screen identifies silencers with distinct chromatin properties and mechanisms of repression. Hofbauer et al.","description":"Cell type-specific gene transcription is a profoundly regulated process that enables development and homeostasis in all animals. Two major classes of distal cis-regulatory DNA elements (CREs), enhancers and silencers, activate or repress transcription, respectively. However, while enhancers have been thoroughly characterized, the sequence and chromatin properties of silencers, as well as their mechanisms of distal transcriptional repression, remain largely unknown. We performed an unbiased genome-wide silencer screen in Drosophila melanogaster S2 cells and discover a novel class of silencers, which recruit one of three different zinc finger transcription factors (TFs) through conserved DNA motifs. Surprisingly, these silencers lack detectable DNA accessibility, placing them outside current chromatin-defined catalogs of CREs (e.g. modENCODE). Further we find that the TF CG11247, which we term Saft, binds to one type of silencers via the orphan DLM3 motif and seems to safeguard cell fate decisions by repressing trachea- and cuticle-specific genes in non- trachea/non-cuticle tissues, such as the ovary or brain. Saft contains the previously unknown, highly conserved effector domain ZAC and functions via the corepressor G9a, yet independently of G9a’s H3K9 methyltransferase activity. Overall, our identification of silencers with unexpected properties and mechanism of action has important implications for the understanding and future study of repressive CREs, as well as the functional annotation of animal genomes.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-11-12T10:07:33.589Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/tkh39ck93y","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/tkh39ck93y"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lorena","last_name":"Hofbauer","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alexander","last_name":"Stark","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/013tf3c58","name":"FWF Austrian Science Fund","grant_id":null},{"id":"https://ror.org/00q32j219","name":"Boehringer Ingelheim (Germany)","grant_id":null},{"id":"https://ror.org/028jc0449","name":"Austrian Research Promotion Agency","grant_id":null},{"id":"https://ror.org/03tx8dq56","name":"Boehringer Ingelheim Foundation","grant_id":null}],"categories":["Genomics","Transcription","Gene Expression"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"2tv7ntcgh6","title":"cisplatin-damaged gene locus-replicate 1-Blank-R2","description":"Cisplatin is a DNA-targeting chemotherapeutic, yet its damaged gene loci are largely unexplored. We utilized a forward chemical genetics strategy to map 7578 cisplatin-damaged genes (CDGs) with >12 fold-enrichment from A549 human lung cancer cells, of which the highly associated signalling pathways include sperm motility, molecular mechanism of cancer, and protein kinase A signalling. The three most frequently cisplatinated penta-nucleotide motifs and genomic region are CTGGG, CCAGG and GCTGG, and promoter, respectively. Among the CDGs are 1330 enzyme, 747 transcription regulator and 486 transporter genes. Importantly, cisplatin targeted 306 protein kinase genes, accounting for 59% of putative protein kinase genes in the human genome, and 92 protein phosphatase genes. This indicates that cisplatin genome-wide disturbs protein phosphorylation. This data set contains the original sequencing data of two biological replicates, matching results to the hg38 genome, and the analysis results of the distribution of the damage areas identified by the two sequencing results.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-06-23T16:24:02.941Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/2tv7ntcgh6","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/2tv7ntcgh6"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Fuyi","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Gene"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"dmyfkn7pnz","title":"cisplatin-damaged gene locus-replicate 2-Blank-R1","description":"Cisplatin is a DNA-targeting chemotherapeutic, yet its damaged gene loci are largely unexplored. We utilized a forward chemical genetics strategy to map 7578 cisplatin-damaged genes (CDGs) with >12 fold-enrichment from A549 human lung cancer cells, of which the highly associated signalling pathways include sperm motility, molecular mechanism of cancer, and protein kinase A signalling. The three most frequently cisplatinated penta-nucleotide motifs and genomic region are CTGGG, CCAGG and GCTGG, and promoter, respectively. Among the CDGs are 1330 enzyme, 747 transcription regulator and 486 transporter genes. Importantly, cisplatin targeted 306 protein kinase genes, accounting for 59% of putative protein kinase genes in the human genome, and 92 protein phosphatase genes. This indicates that cisplatin genome-wide disturbs protein phosphorylation. This data set contains the original sequencing data of two biological replicates, matching results to the hg38 genome, and the analysis results of the distribution of the damage areas identified by the two sequencing results.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-06-23T16:25:54.448Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/dmyfkn7pnz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/dmyfkn7pnz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Fuyi","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Gene"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":139802,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":158,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":139802,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":139061,"restricted":69,"embargoed":672},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2306},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1452},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":664},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":476},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":452},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":435},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":422},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":389},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":328},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":275}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":137149},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1908},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":187},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":139},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":79},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":65},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":57},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":43},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":31},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":937},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":906},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":528},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":407},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":362}]}}